[Applause]  that's members of the group figure  skating in harlem congratulating olympic  gold medalist nathan chan on his  historic win at the winter games in  beijing chen's mesmerizing performance  dominated the men's finger skating short  program he's known as the quad king for  his impressive quadruple spins chen  became the first asian-american man to  meddle in figure skating singles at the  olympics and he's the first american to  do so in more than a decade and nathan  joins us now in studio nathan good  morning and congratulations thank you  thanks for having me uh the quad king  the other day in my living room this is  true i stood there and tried to do just  one step  how'd that go with no skates on and a  hardwood floor it didn't work out well  so congratulations amazing performance  you seem to be having the time of your  life which is what's interesting about  it how are you feeling now  i feel great um it's been about two  weeks since i competed and a lot of  things have gone on during that period  of time you know there's a lot of media  and a lot of things that are associated  with the olympics but it's been a really  fun ride so far has it settled uh i mean  you've got the metal there around your  neck does it feel real yet  definitely this whole experience has  felt very surreal you know i've dreamed  about this moment for a long time um and  you can never really plan for exactly  what it'll feel like but you know being  able to see some of my family yesterday  and uh  just being able to talk to them  throughout the experience of the games  has been really special i i talked with  you and pyeongchang  after you had lost and not performed the  way that you had wanted to  you said you were going to use that as a  learning experience and how you approach  these games so how did you use that in  the lead up here i definitely think i  mean i learned the most from my mistakes  from the things that i don't do quite  right um and so  2018 i think was a really great  learning experience for me um taught me  how to sort of like set my mind for  competitions how to approach  competitions a lot of what we when we're  competing we're only on the ice for six  minutes roughly you know with including  short program and long program but  there's like hours and hours of training  that goes into it to those six minutes  so making sure you understand how to you  know utilize those hours before the  competitions to the best of your  abilities allows you to perform better  in competition you also though and tony  said that like you had so much fun and  even then even when you didn't place  where you wanted to you seem to  appreciate that moment how are you able  to do that  i certainly think over the past four  years um i've been able to come to  skating with a place of gratitude um and  just like recognizing how lucky and  fortunate i am to have these  opportunities to compete to represent  the us to do all these things that i  love so much you know i spent all my  life uh practicing figure skating but  it's the one place where i feel like  truly at home so to then have the  opportunities to do that  a scale in which that i've been dreaming  about for a long time is really special  you said michelle kwan and christie  yamaguchi uh they're your inspiration  two fellow asian american skaters and  you're the the first men to meddle um  gold medal and men's single  what can we do to diversify the sport  i think we're definitely doing the right  things um and  uh as mentioned earlier with figure  skating in harlem where you know there's  organizations like this that are helping  diversify the sport and it allows  skating to be an accessible sport for  everyone um and  teaming with actually was quite diverse  this time having you know chloe kim  myself vincent joe karen chan and many  others that i'm leaving out um but you  know it's great to be able to have this  body of asian american representation at  the olympics and i think that in itself  is so important can we talk more about  how you get your mind right before the  performance we've been talking about  when you're obsessed i'm kind of  obsessed with it so we know music is  part of it and clearly you like music in  your performance sure  what songs in particular do you use  pre-show to get ready  it really depends on where i'm at  physically i suppose i think it also  depends on where i'm what time i'm  competing i figure if it's like morning  or like where i feel like i need to get  jazzed up a little bit more um i've  traditionally listened to like songs  from eminem from 88 rising um edm songs  loud luxury uh stuff like that to just  get me amped up a little bit as i get  closer to competition or if it's like  later in the day where i'm already  pretty amped up and jazzed jazzed up i  need something that calms me down a  little bit more so like patrick watson  things that are like a lot more slow and  like more  melodic are things that like just help  me get in my zone and then rocket man is  what you perform too right uh and elton  john congratulated you  really love the song are you hearing the  song are you in the performance  well i train this program every single  day many times a day so the music is  like ingrained in my head at this point  in time so um i know where the basic  with the nuances of the music are and  i'm pretty comfortable with the music  but certainly the music especially when  you're in a situation where you know  you're stressed out you're nervous and  then also you get like halfway through  the program you get pretty fatigued  having the music sort of elevate you and  give you energy is really helpful well  eminem's uh song lose yourself is an  athlete's go-to song absolutely for the  pre-game um but that's a great question  that tony asked about the moment are you  an individual that completely loses  yourself and after it's done you come to  or  are you completely aware of every single  thing the smell the feel the temperature  of the moment it's definitely different  there are times where i find that we  enter like this flow state where  everything just locks off and like  you're just like so  just in this trance you know throughout  the program but those are the times  where i find i skate the best when i'm  in that state but it's not always there  and i think that this time around like i  definitely wasn't in that specific flow  state but i've also learned to adapt to  when i don't feel that flow state and  still  try to have confidence knowing that i  can still do my elements even if i can't  reach that zone i think people knew in  2018 we'd see you back in 2022.  people are already wondering and i know  you've been asked what about 2026 is  this something that you can see because  that dedication in the four years in  between is something most of us can't  even understand  i definitely think i need to think about  it a little bit more 2026 of course is a  very exciting olympics in milan i think  that'll be really cool experience to  have um however you know i just got off  2022 you know i still have four years we  don't care we want to know now  let him relax dana for sure totally fair  but i mean i would it would be very  exciting but right now you know i'm just  embracing this moment and then trying to  live in this present and figure out what  what's to come in the next couple years  is there a deciding factor is there for  you like i need to know by this time if  i'm really gonna do this i think it's  just a progression of time you know it  depends on how you know this next couple  months ago and then i go back to school  in august and then how school goes and  whether it's something that i'm like hey  i'm like just really invested in school  and i don't really consider anything  else or i'm like hey i'm missing this  aspect of my life so it really depends i  think it's just like a day-to-day sort  of process and i don't really want to  put a concrete you know answer on it for  myself what are you majoring in in  school uh statistics and data science  nice i'd take the medal to class if  you'll ever need an hour that should do  it no doubt about it well we'll see you  in milan okay  for the pasta nothing else fair enough  thank you so much

